// basedpyright's settings, at the repo root rather than in backend/pyproject.toml.
//
// A language server searches its worktree root for a config and does not look into
// subdirectories, so with the settings one level down in backend/ it found none. That
// is what made the difference: basedpyright ignores a user's own typeCheckingMode ONLY
// when a pyrightconfig.json or a pyproject.toml with a [tool.basedpyright] section is
// found. With neither, the editor's settings win - and a developer whose global Zed or
// VS Code config sets "recommended" got that instead of this repo's "strict".
//
// "recommended" is a DIFFERENT rule set, not a smaller one: it enables
// reportUnusedCallResult, reportAny, reportImplicitOverride and more, and sets
// failOnWarnings. The editor then reported code CI is happy with, such as the discarded
// return of response.headers.setdefault() in create_app().
//
// So this file is not only about where the settings live, it is what makes them
// authoritative over whatever each contributor has set globally.
//
// Pointing the server at the old location through Zed's
// basedpyright.analysis.configFilePath was tried twice and does not work - a directory
// is rejected ("Config file ... could not be read") and a file path is ignored - so the
// config moved to where every editor already looks. Nothing editor-specific is needed
// now, in Zed or anywhere else.
//
// pyrightconfig.json takes precedence over pyproject.toml, so [tool.basedpyright] was
// REMOVED from backend/pyproject.toml rather than left behind, where it would have been
// dead config that still read as live. ruff and pytest stay there; the type checker is
// the one tool an editor runs continuously against the whole worktree, which is what
// makes it the exception.
//
// `pixi run typecheck` runs from the repo root with no cwd, so CI and the editor read
// this same file. Comments are fine here: basedpyright parses this as JSONC.
{
  // Only the backend is Python. The frontend is checked by tsc.
  "include": ["backend/src", "backend/tests"],

  "typeCheckingMode": "strict",

  // The package lives under src/, so tests importing expense_tracker resolve without
  // relying on the editable install being present.
  "extraPaths": ["backend/src"]
}
